Derek Ray wrote:

> That is not what leads to the crashes, as extinctionists don't seem to
> run into the problem at all AFAIK.  I haven't dug into the problem to
> figure out exactly what's going on, but from what I know so far, I'm
> going to say it's eventual memory issues related to having 10,000+ items
> on a single square (deathdrops, see).

That's because extinctionists usually stop to polypile the items 
generated. If they wouldn't they would get the same crash.

The reason for the crash is running out of heap memory. It's not items 
on a square it's the size of the whole level in memory. So it is the 
items but they can be anywhere on the level and the crash would ensue.

> Well, if we assume that what is making the game crash is the ability to
> put far too many items on a single square, is the problem with the case
> that generates the ridiculously out-of-bounds circumstance, or the fact
> that you can generally find a way to crash _any_ program if you don't
> close the holes where people can do this type of thing?  And is the
> appropriate fix the one that requires a small effort (and small amount
> of code) to close the single case, or the one that requires a great deal
> of effort to build infrastructure and new code to prop up the single case?

It would mean having a heap monitor. There isn't one as:

1. It requires coding.
2. It slows the game.
3. It is not normally needed.

If a player does not clear the items out the game will crash. Take it as 
a design that prevents scripted puddingfarming having a timelimit.


